WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.720
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:06.410
Do you ever get the feeling that people are
talking about you behind your back?

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.400
Well that’s because they are.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.539
Pretty much every shop, restaurant and bar
in the world uses a system of secret codes

00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:19.570
to communicate between staff members without
alerting customers to an alarming situation,

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:24.230
or to simply insult customers without them
realising.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:29.500
Transport for London, often called TFL, have
been using secret codes in abundance for a

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.500
long time.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.840
As one can imagine, being in charge of all
public transport in the city of London means

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.960
there are literally hundreds of issues to
deal with on a daily basis.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.460
From someone ejecting their dinner on the
bus or something more serious such as terrorism

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.020
– TFL have a code for it.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.260
As you would expect from a public transport
operator, most of their codes are used to

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.180
request clean-ups of various bodily fluids
ejected from commuters.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:04.190
To start from the beginning, code 1 means
blood, code 2, urine and/or feces, code 3,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.490
vomit, code 4, general spillage, which is
probably the least disgusting on this list,

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:15.470
code 5 is broken glass, code 6 is litter and
finally a code 7 is anything that doesn’t

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.280
fit into the previous categories.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.140
You know, for those odd times when something
comes out your body and you think to yourself

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.840
“what the hell is that”.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:30.030
But TFL have a far more serious secret code…
by the name of “Inspector Sands”.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:35.390
Which is used to alert the police and other
authorities to either a fire or a bomb scare,

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:38.030
without causing the general public to panic.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:57.270
This is a recording of the chilling announcement
played in an underground tube station.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:04.579
If the issue is not investigated within 3
minutes, then the station will be automatically

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:06.380
evacuated.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.620
The message was played on repeat across all
London tube stations during the 7/7 London

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.800
bombings.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.050
The world’s largest retailer Wal-Mart, has
a whole array of staff codes that are blasted

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.840
over the tannoys, in various situations.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.380
From Code Blue which is a bomb threat to Code
Green which means there’s a hostage situation.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:31.790
But Wal-Mart is best known for creating a
code that is now widely used by thousands

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.040
of establishments across America – Code
Adam.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:42.459
In 1981 a 6-year-old boy named Adam was abducted
in Sears department store in Florida.

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:46.489
Public service announcements were made across
the store that Adam was lost.

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:50.850
After a long search he was found decapitated.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:56.610
In memory of Adam, Wal-Mart introduced code
Adam to signal a lost child in one of their

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:57.610
stores.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.709
But it’s more than just a code, there’s
a step-by-step system that all employees are

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:03.349
trained to follow.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:09.150
If a child is reported missing “code Adam”
is announced across the store and all exterior

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.410
exits are immediately locked.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:16.480
A description of the missing child is paged
to all store employees and they will begin

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.910
a search.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.660
If the child is not found within 10 minutes,
then law enforcement is called.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:28.090
The doors are only unlocked once the child
is found unharmed or law enforcement takes

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.090
over the case.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:36.280
The system works so well that in 2003 congress
passed legislation that made it a legal requirement

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:41.580
for all federal buildings in the US to implement
Code Adam.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.730
Another code that signifies imminent danger
is “Time Check” – it’s used commonly

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.920
in department stores in the UK, specifically
John Lewis and IKEA.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.659
It means there’s an ongoing bomb scare.

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:59.099
If employees hear “Time Check” announced,
then they must immediately stop what they’re

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:03.989
doing and search for suspicious packages or
unattended bags.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:08.050
Emergency codes are even used away from dry
land.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:12.799
Even when there are no icebergs about there’s
still a lot that can go wrong on a cruise

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:13.799
ship.

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:18.809
And with the average age of cruise ship passengers
being over 50, they are basically floating

00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:20.039
nursing homes.

00:04:20.039 --> 00:04:25.990
So it’s no surprise that over 200 people
die on cruise ships each year.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:31.169
To accommodate this all cruise ships actually
have morgues on board.

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:36.860
But what happens when someone passes away,
do the crew members hastily wrap them up in

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:40.199
bed linen and push them to the morgue on the
drinks trolley?

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:43.129
No, they have a secret code of course.

00:04:43.129 --> 00:04:48.680
“Operation Rising Star” is broadcast across
the vessel when a passenger has passed away.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:55.069
And if you hear “Operation Bright Star”
that signals a medical emergency.

00:04:55.069 --> 00:04:59.229
Emergency codes are also heavily utilised
up in the sky.

00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:03.909
Airlines use secret codes to communicate more
frequently than most other companies.

00:05:03.909 --> 00:05:07.569
You’ll often see cabin crew uttering gibberish
into walkie talkies.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:13.199
One of the most serious is a code 7500 which
means the plane has been hijacked.

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:19.349
There are other codes such as a 7600 which
indicates a radio failure, but there’s also

00:05:19.349 --> 00:05:23.770
a code 7700 which means a general emergency.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:28.849
So it could be anything from the toilet being
blocked to a couple of holiday makers panicking

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:31.749
because they left their son Kevin at the airport.

00:05:31.749 --> 00:05:37.639
But rather strangely there’s also “Code
Bravo” which is the complete opposite of

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:44.069
all the other codes we’ve discussed, because
it’s intended to intentionally cause panic.

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:49.750
A flight attendant will repeatedly shout “code
bravo” loudly if they want passengers to

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:54.659
panic in order to distract their attention
away from something more serious going on,

00:05:54.659 --> 00:05:59.979
on the plane, that needs to be dealt with,
without interference.

00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:05.240
Of course, secret codes can be used to make
fun of people without them having any idea

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.300
what you’re going on about.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:12.990
IT departments across the world share a common
code that’s often used when assisting muggles

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.979
with their never ending computer problems.

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:22.759
The code is PEBKAC and it stands for “Problem
Exists Between Keyboard and Chair” – in

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:26.409
other words, the computer isn’t broken,
you are.

00:06:26.409 --> 00:06:31.800
IT professionals have been known to write
this code on incident report forms the world

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:32.800
over.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:39.399
Oh and lasty, bartenders across the world
have a large numerical code system, such as

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:46.580
a code 300, which means a guest needs assistance,
or 86, meaning a product is out of stock.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:51.939
If you’re in a bar and the bartenders say
“690” it means a hot girl just walked

00:06:51.939 --> 00:06:52.939
in.

00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:58.659
So there you go, you now know all the secret
codes intended to stop you from panicking

00:06:58.659 --> 00:07:11.250
in an emergency, so in the future you can
now be suitably panicked; you’re welcome.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:17.979
Thanks for watching.

